% refs.bib -- bibliography.  Only entries actually cited belong here
% (CLAUDE.md, Phase 3).
%
% KEYS: follow the alpha labels of the target paper's own bibliography, so the
% reader can match our citations against the paper directly.  Record the
% decision in spec.md, "confirmed conventions".
%
% ASCII ONLY.  Everything under tex/ must be ASCII (see CLAUDE.md).
%
% BEWARE: bibtex has no comment character.  Lines outside an entry are
% ignored, but an "at" sign anywhere -- including in prose like this -- starts
% an entry and derails the parse.  Never write the entry types with their
% sigil in a comment; that is why they appear bare below.
%
% TITLE BRACING.  bibtex's alpha style lowercases the title of article,
% incollection and misc entries, and it does NOT protect math: "On $L^2$
% extension" prints as "On $l^2$ extension".  No warning, no error, and
% tex/check.sh cannot see it -- only reading main.bbl does.  Wrap math and
% proper nouns in braces: {$L^2$}, {O}hsawa--{T}akegoshi.  Book titles are
% not lowercased.  Read main.bbl once whenever a chapter is finalised.
%
% NO working notes in the note field -- the alpha style PRINTS it, and once
% put twelve pages of our acquisition notes into the final PDF.  Acquisition
% status lives in refs/NOTES.md.  Only what helps the reader chase a citation
% belongs in note (an arXiv identifier; how the target paper misnames the
% entry).

@book{Har77,
  author    = {Robin Hartshorne},
  title     = {Algebraic Geometry},
  series    = {Graduate Texts in Mathematics},
  volume    = {52},
  publisher = {Springer},
  year      = {1977}
}
